WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.605
This video explains how a product image is assembled with Yocto, and how to live with chip vendor BSPs without drowning in patches.

00:00:08.005 --> 00:00:19.707
Yocto / BitBake: A build system that compiles a whole custom Linux image from recipes; BitBake is its build engine. Like: A bakery that follows recipe cards to bake a whole custom cake from scratch.

00:00:20.807 --> 00:00:24.894
The chip vendor sends a new kit with a kernel carrying two thousand patches.

00:00:25.144 --> 00:00:31.866
Kernel: The core of the operating system that controls memory, devices and which program runs. Like: The building manager who owns every key and decides who uses which room.

00:00:32.116 --> 00:00:35.704
Your product has its own fifty patches on top of the old kit.

00:00:35.954 --> 00:00:40.853
How do you move without breaking everything? That's what build systems and good layering are for.

00:00:41.903 --> 00:00:48.625
A vendor BSP is like a meal kit: everything in the box, pre measured, but designed for the vendor's kitchen.

00:00:49.025 --> 00:00:55.782
BSP: Board Support Package: the chip vendor's bundle of bootloader, kernel, drivers and settings for one chip or board. Like: The starter kit that comes with a new appliance: parts, manual and tools for that exact model.

00:00:56.082 --> 00:01:02.038
Yocto is your recipe book, organized in layers. Each layer adds or changes recipes.

00:01:02.438 --> 00:01:09.416
A b b append file says: use the vendor's recipe, but add our spices. You never edit the kit itself.

00:01:09.816 --> 00:01:14.704
And pinning every layer revision means you can cook exactly the same meal again next year.

00:01:15.904 --> 00:01:16.600
Words.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.540
BitBake is Yocto's engine. It reads recipes and runs their tasks.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:27.443
A recipe describes how to fetch, patch, configure, build and package one component.

00:01:27.843 --> 00:01:35.401
A layer is a folder of recipes, like meta qcom from Qualcomm, meta freescale for N X P, and your company's own layer.

00:01:35.801 --> 00:01:40.387
The shared state cache stores build outputs so unchanged parts are never rebuilt.

00:01:40.787 --> 00:01:46.058
Upstream means the main open source project. Downstream means a vendor or company fork of it.

00:01:47.258 --> 00:01:49.290
Here's how an image comes together.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:54.020
You start with poky, the Yocto reference, plus the vendor's layers.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.378
On top, your company layer adds b b append files and kernel config fragments.

00:01:59.778 --> 00:02:03.713
BitBake parses every recipe and builds a dependency graph.

00:02:04.113 --> 00:02:12.368
For each recipe, it fetches source, applies patches, configures and compiles. Anything unchanged comes from the shared state cache.

00:02:12.768 --> 00:02:14.939
Outputs are split into packages.

00:02:15.339 --> 00:02:20.192
Then the root filesystem, kernel and device tree are combined into a flashable image.

00:02:20.592 --> 00:02:27.256
Device tree: A text map of the hardware on a board (which chips, on which pins and addresses) that the kernel reads at boot. Like: The floor plan of a house, so the electrician knows where every socket is without digging into walls.

00:02:27.556 --> 00:02:30.679
And you flash it, or run it in QEMU with run qemu.

00:02:31.079 --> 00:02:39.392
QEMU: A program that emulates a whole computer, so you can test kernels without real hardware. Like: A flight simulator for software.

00:02:40.492 --> 00:02:42.025
Set up the build environment.

00:02:42.425 --> 00:02:43.551
Build an image.

00:02:43.951 --> 00:02:48.920
Open a shell inside the kernel recipe's work directory, with the cross compiler ready.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.453
Change kernel options interactively. Then save them as a fragment.

00:02:53.853 --> 00:03:00.018
Dev tool modify checks out the source for editing, and dev tool finish turns your commits into patches in your layer.

00:03:00.418 --> 00:03:06.978
Bitbake dash e shows the final value of any variable, which answers most where did this come from questions.

00:03:07.378 --> 00:03:15.156
And this line builds and boots an ARM sixty four image in QEMU, with no hardware. Expect a long first build and a lot of disk space.

00:03:16.356 --> 00:03:19.050
Now a senior strategy for vendor BSPs.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:23.072
Always know exactly which vendor release each product is on.

00:03:23.472 --> 00:03:30.055
Keep your changes as a numbered patch series on top. Moving to the next vendor release becomes a mechanical rebase.

00:03:30.455 --> 00:03:38.895
Before patching a vendor driver, search upstream. The fix often exists. Backport it, with its fixes tag, instead of inventing your own.

00:03:39.295 --> 00:03:43.916
Driver: Kernel code that knows how to operate one kind of hardware. Like: A translator who speaks one specific machine's language.

00:03:44.166 --> 00:03:50.633
File vendor tickets with a minimal reproduction, versions, logs, and a clear ask. That saves weeks.

00:03:51.033 --> 00:03:56.803
And keep a BSP delta document: what changed, why, and whether it can go upstream.

00:03:58.003 --> 00:04:03.599
Question for you: Why put your product changes in your own layer instead of editing the vendor's files?

00:04:08.899 --> 00:04:17.351
Answer: So a new vendor release can be dropped in and your changes reapplied cleanly, and it's always clear which changes are yours.

00:04:18.451 --> 00:04:19.148
Recap.

00:04:19.548 --> 00:04:21.696
Never edit vendor layers in place.

00:04:22.096 --> 00:04:26.449
Put changes in your own layer, as b b appends and config fragments.

00:04:26.849 --> 00:04:28.475
Pin every layer revision.

00:04:28.875 --> 00:04:31.603
Prefer upstream backports to custom fixes.

00:04:32.003 --> 00:04:33.698
And document every delta.
